-          "text": "the house the 1911 sheet labels \"1918\" over \"(1936)\", which the sheet 28 census rules is the Thompson/Allerton house (ruling r28-allerton: one villa on the drawn footprint, the bare 1936 corner its lawn, no house at 1916 or 1930), is no longer a district draft. It stands, under the draft's own id 1936 Prairie Avenue — the Thompson/Allerton house (Italianate, L. B. Dixon, 1869-70), as a k01 frontage record built by the k01 emit generator: Daniel M. Thompson's 1869-70 Italian villa by L. B. Dixon, Samuel W. Allerton's from 1879 and still his family's in 1904, razed in 1915. **What is read, not invented.** From the 1911 Sanborn sheet 28 (trace s28fp-1918-b1): the main block, the \"bay4B\" tower on the front at v 5.95-9.74, the reception room's half-octagonal bay and the dining range on the south flank, the library's canted bay on the north, the \"rear2B\" range behind. From the Chicago Tribune of 30 October 1869 ('A Private Palace. Daniel Thompson's $100,000 Dwelling, read through Chicagology's transcription and held as inferred until the Tribune's page is read): the brownstone ashlar basement 6 ft high under Baltimore pressed brick with brownstone trim; the main floor 6 ft over grade; the principal and second storeys 14 ft 9 in and 13 ft 6 in; the cornice's top at 44 ft and the rear part's at 37 ft; the tower 73 ft; the entrance through the tower under a stone porch on fluted Corinthian columns; the verandah before the reception room's two front windows, its roof a balustraded balcony; the parlor's two front windows; the attic's frieze lights. From the undated winter photograph ([1936 S. Prairie Ave. (Daniel M. Thompson / Samuel W. Allerton residence).]…, rights check required: forms and places only): the bracketed cornice, the low hip with its railed deck, the stacks, the iron fence on its curb. **What is invented.** The walls' thickness; the brick's panel (K03's rough red-brown stands for the Tribune's Baltimore pressed brick, which the kit has no panel for); the roof's covering (the Tribune's slate is laid by K04 only at 45 degrees and over, and the photograph's hip is low, so it is the kit's painted standing seam with the Tribune's copper flashings); the roof's 25 degree pitch and the deck's 15.0 m height; every window's place along the flanks and the rear, and the sashes' sizes; the north stack; the gates, the walks and the rainwater goods. **What is built otherwise than the evidence, and why.** The front stands on the north block's line (u 17.7): the sheet sets the part south of the tower 0.95 m behind it, under the verandah, and K01's main body is one rectangle. The tower is square, as the photograph and the sheet draw it (K08's polygonal tower with four sides, a face to the street, 3.79 m across and its front 1.3 m out of the wall, the sheet's), but its back stands on the front wall's plane all the way up, so over the main roof it is a 1.3 m deep shaft and not the free-standing square the photograph shows above the cornice; its cornice is the kit's plain eaves, without the photograph's consoles (K08 adds no brackets to a tower), and its belvedere rises flush from the shaft rather than set back over a corbelled balcony. The door stands in the front's bay just north of the tower, its stoop running straight to the avenue: the Tribune enters through the tower and the photograph's flight runs north along the front, but K01 refuses a door behind a tower. The verandah's fluted columns are the corner porch's plain piers. The rear range is built to its second storey's head (9.45 m) under a flat roof, short of the Tribune's 37 ft cornice."
