as no panel for); the roof's covering (the Tribune's slate is laid by K04 only at 45 degrees and over, and the photograph's hip is low, so it is the kit's painted standing seam with the Tribune's copper flashings); the roof's 25 degree pitch and the deck's 15.0 m height; every window's place along the flanks and the rear, and the sashes' sizes; the north stack; the gates, the walks and the rainwater goods. **What is built otherwise than the evidence, and why.** The front stands on the north block's line (u 17.7): the sheet sets the part south of the tower 0.95 m behind it, under the verandah, and K01's main body is one rectangle. The tower is square, as the photograph and the sheet draw it (K08's polygonal tower with four sides, a face to the street, 3.79 m across and its front 1.3 m out of the wall, the sheet's), but its back stands on the front wall's plane all the way up, so over the main roof it is a 1.3 m deep shaft and not the free-standing square the photograph shows above the cornice. Its belvedere's cornice carries a console at each free corner and a row of plain brackets along its faces, and the belvedere stands back over a corbelled stone balcony round its cheeks and front, as the photograph draws them; their sizes (the balcony 0.55 m out on a 0.3 m slab and three 0.16 m courses, a 0.75 m balustrade; consoles 0.9 m deep; brackets 0.37 m apart) are read through the photograph's scale at the belvedere's lights and are reconstructed, and the photograph's carved consoles are the kit's plain quarter-ellipse ones and its moulded corbel table the kit's stepped courses. Both balconies stop on the wall's plane with the shaft. The bracketed cornice and balcony at the main cornice's level are not built: the shaft passes the main cornice with a plain string course. The door stands in the front's bay just north of the tower, its stoop running straight to the avenue: the Tribune enters through the tower and the photograph's flight runs north along the front, but K01 re